1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,408
A door swings on its hinge.

2
00:00:02,408 --> 00:00:04,576
A fan blade spins on its axle.

3
00:00:04,576 --> 00:00:06,533
A wheel turns on a bicycle.

4
00:00:06,533 --> 00:00:07,652
Nothing breaks.

5
00:00:07,652 --> 00:00:09,440
Only the position changes.

6
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:11,466
Molecules can do this too.

7
00:00:11,466 --> 00:00:15,175
In ethane, the single bond between
the two carbons is the axle.

8
00:00:15,625 --> 00:00:19,463
Aarav spins the front wheel of his
bicycle in the college corridor.

9
00:00:19,463 --> 00:00:21,471
The spokes keep passing the fork.

10
00:00:21,821 --> 00:00:24,757
One moment a spoke hides
right behind the fork.

11
00:00:24,757 --> 00:00:26,982
The next moment it sits in a gap.

12
00:00:27,482 --> 00:00:31,267
By the end, you will know why ethane
prefers one way of sitting, and by

13
00:00:31,267 --> 00:00:32,167
how much.

14
00:00:32,578 --> 00:00:34,293
So what is a conformation?

15
00:00:34,643 --> 00:00:37,870
In ethane, two carbons are
joined by one sigma bond.

16
00:00:37,870 --> 00:00:40,197
Each carbon also holds three hydrogens.

17
00:00:40,547 --> 00:00:42,674
A sigma bond is like an axle.

18
00:00:42,674 --> 00:00:46,085
One carbon can spin, and
the bond does not break.

19
00:00:46,435 --> 00:00:50,117
Each new arrangement made by this
spin is called a conformation.

20
00:00:50,517 --> 00:00:52,037
There are endless conformations.

21
00:00:52,037 --> 00:00:52,937
Two are special.

22
00:00:52,932 --> 00:00:56,445
In the eclipsed form, the back hydrogens
hide right behind the front ones.

23
00:00:56,845 --> 00:01:00,515
In the staggered form, the back hydrogens
sit in the gaps between the front

24
00:01:00,515 --> 00:01:01,415
ones.

25
00:01:01,598 --> 00:01:03,593
How do we draw this on paper?

26
00:01:03,593 --> 00:01:04,949
There are two ways.

27
00:01:04,949 --> 00:01:06,963
First, the sawhorse projection.

28
00:01:07,313 --> 00:01:10,186
Look at the carbon carbon
bond from an angle.

29
00:01:10,186 --> 00:01:12,284
Draw it as a long slanted line.

30
00:01:12,284 --> 00:01:16,915
The front carbon sits at the lower
end, the back carbon at the upper end.

31
00:01:17,315 --> 00:01:20,352
Each carbon carries three
hydrogens, drawn as short lines.

32
00:01:20,752 --> 00:01:22,633
Second, the Newman projection.

33
00:01:22,633 --> 00:01:25,962
Now look straight down the
carbon carbon bond, head on.

34
00:01:26,362 --> 00:01:28,200
The front carbon is a point.

35
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:30,413
Its three bonds meet at the centre.

36
00:01:30,813 --> 00:01:34,517
The back carbon hides behind
it, so we draw it as a circle.

37
00:01:34,517 --> 00:01:37,141
Its bonds start at the edge of the circle.

38
00:01:37,541 --> 00:01:39,172
Now spin the back carbon.

39
00:01:39,172 --> 00:01:40,142
Watch closely.

40
00:01:40,142 --> 00:01:44,355
Every sixty degrees, the molecule
switches between eclipsed and staggered.

41
00:01:45,005 --> 00:01:46,501
Which form is more stable?

42
00:01:46,501 --> 00:01:48,790
Think of the bonds as clouds of electrons.

43
00:01:49,190 --> 00:01:53,519
In the eclipsed form, the bond clouds
of the front and back face each other,

44
00:01:53,519 --> 00:01:56,125
like two crowds meeting
in a narrow corridor.

45
00:01:56,125 --> 00:01:57,025
They push.

46
00:01:57,286 --> 00:02:01,408
In the staggered form, the clouds
sit as far apart as they can.

47
00:02:01,408 --> 00:02:02,308
Less push.

48
00:02:02,646 --> 00:02:03,728
Pause and think.

49
00:02:03,728 --> 00:02:06,928
Which form has the lower
energy, eclipsed or staggered?

50
00:02:10,128 --> 00:02:11,028
Staggered!

51
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:15,370
Its bond clouds are farthest apart,
so it has the least push, the lowest

52
00:02:15,370 --> 00:02:17,467
energy, and the most stability.

53
00:02:18,117 --> 00:02:20,450
Let's measure this push, link by link.

54
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:21,700
Link one.

55
00:02:21,584 --> 00:02:25,773
The push between the bond clouds of
the two carbons is called torsional

56
00:02:25,773 --> 00:02:26,673
strain.

57
00:02:27,154 --> 00:02:28,054
Link two.

58
00:02:27,874 --> 00:02:31,761
The eclipsed form has the most
torsional strain, so it has the highest

59
00:02:31,761 --> 00:02:32,661
energy.

60
00:02:32,340 --> 00:02:34,323
The staggered form has the least.

61
00:02:35,073 --> 00:02:35,973
Link three.

62
00:02:35,912 --> 00:02:38,447
Plot the energy as the back carbon turns.

63
00:02:38,447 --> 00:02:42,613
The curve rises and falls every sixty
degrees, like hills and valleys.

64
00:02:43,363 --> 00:02:44,263
Link four.

65
00:02:44,128 --> 00:02:47,932
The height of each hill is only about
twelve point five kilojoules per

66
00:02:47,932 --> 00:02:48,832
mole.

67
00:02:48,424 --> 00:02:50,726
This is the energy barrier to rotation.

68
00:02:51,476 --> 00:02:52,376
Link five.

69
00:02:52,222 --> 00:02:53,542
This barrier is tiny.

70
00:02:53,542 --> 00:02:57,373
At room temperature, molecules bump
into each other hard enough to cross

71
00:02:57,373 --> 00:02:58,479
it all the time.

72
00:02:58,479 --> 00:03:00,596
So we cannot separate the two forms.

73
00:03:01,346 --> 00:03:02,377
Remember this.

74
00:03:02,377 --> 00:03:04,245
Same bonds, different spin.

75
00:03:04,245 --> 00:03:06,412
Staggered is the comfortable seat.

76
00:03:07,112 --> 00:03:08,733
Now, one worked example.

77
00:03:08,733 --> 00:03:12,981
In one full turn of the back carbon,
how many eclipsed and staggered forms

78
00:03:12,981 --> 00:03:15,816
appear, and what does
climbing the hills cost?

79
00:03:16,116 --> 00:03:17,016
Step one.

80
00:03:16,845 --> 00:03:20,978
The angle between a front bond and
a back bond, in the Newman view, is

81
00:03:20,978 --> 00:03:22,569
called the dihedral angle.

82
00:03:23,319 --> 00:03:24,219
Step two.

83
00:03:23,978 --> 00:03:27,701
Eclipsed forms come at zero, one
hundred and twenty, and two hundred and

84
00:03:27,701 --> 00:03:28,601
forty degrees.

85
00:03:29,312 --> 00:03:30,212
Step three.

86
00:03:30,114 --> 00:03:34,098
Staggered forms come at sixty, one
hundred and eighty, and three hundred

87
00:03:34,098 --> 00:03:34,998
degrees.

88
00:03:34,717 --> 00:03:36,697
So, three of each in one turn.

89
00:03:37,447 --> 00:03:38,347
Step four.

90
00:03:38,075 --> 00:03:41,185
Each climb from staggered to eclipsed
costs twelve point five kilojoules

91
00:03:41,185 --> 00:03:42,085
per mole.

92
00:03:41,777 --> 00:03:44,036
For two moles, that is
twenty five kilojoules.

93
00:03:44,786 --> 00:03:48,755
Every form in between, neither eclipsed
nor staggered, is called a skew

94
00:03:48,755 --> 00:03:49,655
form.

95
00:03:49,897 --> 00:03:51,874
Now put the two drawings side by side.

96
00:03:52,274 --> 00:03:56,261
The sawhorse shows the bond from the
side, so you can see both carbons

97
00:03:56,261 --> 00:03:58,002
and the length of the bond.

98
00:03:58,402 --> 00:04:02,515
The Newman view looks down the bond,
so you see the angle between front

99
00:04:02,515 --> 00:04:04,291
and back bonds at a glance.

100
00:04:04,691 --> 00:04:06,693
Same molecule, same form.

101
00:04:06,693 --> 00:04:08,923
Only the camera angle changes.

102
00:04:09,573 --> 00:04:11,034
Now, the exam trap.

103
00:04:11,384 --> 00:04:15,673
Students write that rotation about the
carbon carbon single bond in ethane

104
00:04:15,673 --> 00:04:16,997
is completely free.

105
00:04:17,397 --> 00:04:18,297
Wrong.

106
00:04:17,919 --> 00:04:20,208
It is almost free, but not completely.

107
00:04:20,208 --> 00:04:24,004
A small barrier of about twelve point
five kilojoules per mole stands in

108
00:04:24,004 --> 00:04:24,904
the way.

109
00:04:25,314 --> 00:04:26,759
Where is this asked?

110
00:04:26,759 --> 00:04:30,793
Board papers ask you to draw the
sawhorse and Newman projections.

111
00:04:30,793 --> 00:04:34,216
Entrance tests ask about
stability and torsional strain.

112
00:04:34,816 --> 00:04:35,818
Exam level now.

113
00:04:35,818 --> 00:04:39,791
In a Newman projection of ethane, the
back carbon starts eclipsed and turns

114
00:04:39,791 --> 00:04:41,752
by one hundred and eighty degrees.

115
00:04:41,752 --> 00:04:42,804
What do we get?

116
00:04:42,804 --> 00:04:43,849
Pause and solve.

117
00:04:49,249 --> 00:04:50,592
The answer is B.

118
00:04:50,592 --> 00:04:52,936
Staggered, at the lowest energy.

119
00:04:53,436 --> 00:04:57,291
Eclipsed forms come every one hundred
and twenty degrees: at zero, one

120
00:04:57,291 --> 00:04:58,620
twenty and two forty.

121
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:02,031
One hundred and eighty
is not on that list.

122
00:05:02,031 --> 00:05:06,567
It sits halfway between one twenty
and two forty, so it is staggered.

123
00:05:07,067 --> 00:05:10,982
Staggered is the lowest point on the
curve, twelve point five kilojoules

124
00:05:10,982 --> 00:05:12,462
per mole below eclipsed.

125
00:05:12,462 --> 00:05:15,597
A and D get the form wrong,
and C is a skew form.

126
00:05:16,197 --> 00:05:17,737
Here is your recap.

127
00:05:17,737 --> 00:05:19,320
Screenshot this card.

128
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:24,706
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

129
00:05:25,106 --> 00:05:26,006
One.

130
00:05:25,524 --> 00:05:28,474
In a Newman projection, how
is the back carbon drawn?

131
00:05:29,274 --> 00:05:30,174
Two.

132
00:05:29,655 --> 00:05:32,613
Why is the staggered form more
stable than the eclipsed form?

133
00:05:33,413 --> 00:05:34,313
Three.

134
00:05:33,915 --> 00:05:37,228
Why can we not separate the two
forms of ethane in a bottle?

135
00:05:38,128 --> 00:05:42,013
Next time: why can a double bond
not spin like a single bond?

136
00:05:42,013 --> 00:05:46,103
We find out in alkenes, their
structure, isomerism and preparation.
